var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"examples/functions/#examples-functions","page":"Functions and methods","text":"The docstrings for these abbreviations are in the reference.\n\n\"\"\"\n`foo` is declared with `function foo end`, so this docstring belongs to the function, not\nto one method. [`METHODLIST`](@ref) lists its methods with links to their source:\n\n$(METHODLIST)\n\n---\n\n[`TYPEDMETHODLIST`](@ref) lists the same methods with argument and return types:\n\n$(TYPEDMETHODLIST)\n\"\"\"\nfunction foo end\n\n\"\"\"\nThis method has a default value for `y`, so Julia defines two methods, `foo(x::Int)` and\n`foo(x::Int, y::Int)`. [`SIGNATURES`](@ref) prints both:\n\n$(SIGNATURES)\n\n---\n\n[`TYPEDSIGNATURES`](@ref) adds the argument types and the inferred return type:\n\n$(TYPEDSIGNATURES)\n\n---\n\nPassing `defaults = true` to [`MethodSignatures`](@ref DocStringExtensions.MethodSignatures)\nor [`TypedMethodSignatures`](@ref DocStringExtensions.TypedMethodSignatures) shows the\ndefault value and prints the two methods as one signature:\n\n$(DocStringExtensions.TypedMethodSignatures(true; defaults = true))\n\n---\n\n[`FUNCTIONNAME`](@ref) prints the function's name, here $(FUNCTIONNAME), for use in a\nsignature written by hand:\n\n    $(FUNCTIONNAME)(x, ...)\n\"\"\"\nfoo(x::Int, y::Int=3) = x + y\n\n\"\"\"\nA second method of [`$(FUNCTIONNAME)`](@ref). [`SIGNATURES`](@ref):\n\n$(SIGNATURES)\n\n[`TYPEDSIGNATURES`](@ref):\n\n$(TYPEDSIGNATURES)\n\"\"\"\nfoo(x::String) = length(x)\n\n\n\n\nThe docstrings render as:","title":"Functions and methods"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/functions/#Type-parameters","page":"Functions and methods","text":"TYPEDSIGNATURES rebuilds a signature from Julia's internal representation, so type parameters may print differently from the source:\n\n\"\"\"\nA method for [`$(FUNCTIONNAME)`](@ref) with one type parameter. [`TYPEDSIGNATURES`](@ref):\n\n$(TYPEDSIGNATURES)\n\nFor comparison, [`SIGNATURES`](@ref):\n\n$(SIGNATURES)\n\"\"\"\nbar(x::AbstractArray{T}, y::T) where {T <: Integer} = Int(y)\n\n\"\"\"\nA method for [`$(FUNCTIONNAME)`](@ref) with an unnamed argument. [`TYPEDSIGNATURES`](@ref):\n\n$(TYPEDSIGNATURES)\n\nFor comparison, [`SIGNATURES`](@ref):\n\n$(SIGNATURES)\n\"\"\"\nbar(x::AbstractArray{T}, ::String) where {T <: Integer} = x\n\n\"\"\"\nA method for [`$(FUNCTIONNAME)`](@ref) with two type parameters. [`TYPEDSIGNATURES`](@ref):\n\n$(TYPEDSIGNATURES)\n\nFor comparison, [`SIGNATURES`](@ref):\n\n$(SIGNATURES)\n\"\"\"\nbar(x::AbstractArray{T}, y::U) where {T <: Integer, U <: AbstractFloat} = 0\n\n\n\n\nThe docstrings render as:","title":"Type parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"examples/functions/#Examples.foo","page":"Functions and methods","text":"foo is declared with function foo end, so this docstring belongs to the function, not to one method. METHODLIST lists its methods with links to their source:\n\nfoo(x)\nfoo(x, y)\n\ndefined at src/functions.jl:42.\n\nfoo(x)\n\ndefined at src/functions.jl:53.\n\n\n\nTYPEDMETHODLIST lists the same methods with argument and return types:\n\nfoo(x::Int64) -> Int64\nfoo(x::Int64, y::Int64) -> Int64\n\ndefined at src/functions.jl:42.\n\nfoo(x::String) -> Int64\n\ndefined at src/functions.jl:53.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.foo"},{"category":"method","location":"examples/functions/#Examples.foo-Tuple{Int64}","page":"Functions and methods","text":"This method has a default value for y, so Julia defines two methods, foo(x::Int) and foo(x::Int, y::Int). SIGNATURES prints both:\n\nfoo(x)\nfoo(x, y)\n\n\n\nTYPEDSIGNATURES adds the argument types and the inferred return type:\n\nfoo(x::Int64) -> Int64\nfoo(x::Int64, y::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\n\nPassing defaults = true to MethodSignatures or TypedMethodSignatures shows the default value and prints the two methods as one signature:\n\nfoo(x::Int64, y::Int64=3) -> Int64\n\n\n\nFUNCTIONNAME prints the function's name, here foo, for use in a signature written by hand:\n\nfoo(x, ...)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.foo"},{"category":"method","location":"examples/functions/#Examples.foo-Tuple{String}","page":"Functions and methods","text":"A second method of foo. SIGNATURES:\n\nfoo(x)\n\nTYPEDSIGNATURES:\n\nfoo(x::String) -> Int64\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.foo"},{"category":"method","location":"examples/functions/#Examples.bar-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, T}} where T<:Integer","page":"Functions and methods","text":"A method for bar with one type parameter. TYPEDSIGNATURES:\n\nbar(x::AbstractArray{T}, y::T) where T<:Integer -> Int64\n\nFor comparison, SIGNATURES:\n\nbar(x, y)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.bar"},{"category":"method","location":"examples/functions/#Examples.bar-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, String}} where T<:Integer","page":"Functions and methods","text":"A method for bar with an unnamed argument. TYPEDSIGNATURES:\n\nbar(\n    x::AbstractArray{T},\n    ::String\n) where T<:Integer -> AbstractArray{T} where T<:Integer\n\nFor comparison, SIGNATURES:\n\nbar(x, ::String)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.bar"},{"category":"method","location":"examples/functions/#Examples.bar-Union{Tuple{U}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, U}} where {T<:Integer, U<:AbstractFloat}","page":"Functions and methods","text":"A method for bar with two type parameters. TYPEDSIGNATURES:\n\nbar(\n    x::AbstractArray{T},\n    y::U\n) where {T<:Integer, U<:AbstractFloat} -> Int64\n\nFor comparison, SIGNATURES:\n\nbar(x, y)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.bar"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"These functions, types etc. are internal to DocStringExtensions and not part of the public API. They may change or be removed arbitrarily from version to version.\n\nPages = [\"internals.md\"]","title":"Internals"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.alltypesigs-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"alltypesigs(sig)\n\nReturns a Vector of the Tuple types contained in sig.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.alltypesigs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.append_defaults-Tuple{Any, Any, Method, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"append_defaults(args, kws, method, definition)\n\nAppend =default to each formatted argument in args and keyword in kws that has a default value in definition. Leaves both unchanged when method does not match definition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.append_defaults"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.argument_names-Tuple{Method, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"argument_names(method, definition)\n\nThe positional argument names of method, with each destructured argument written as it appears in definition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.argument_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.argument_types-Tuple{Method, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"argument_types(method, n)\n\nThe declared types of the first n positional arguments of method, with a vararg argument's element type in place of its Vararg. A vararg with a count, such as xs::Vararg{Int,2}, gets Any.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.argument_types"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.arguments-Tuple{Method}","page":"Internals","text":"arguments(m)\n\nReturns the list of arguments for a particular method m.\n\nExamples\n\nf(x; a = 1, b...) = x\nargs = arguments(first(methods(f)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.arguments"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.cleanpath-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Internals","text":"cleanpath(path)\n\nRemove the depot prefix from a file path inside a depot, such as an installed package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.cleanpath"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.collapse_defaults-Tuple{Any, Nothing}","page":"Internals","text":"collapse_defaults(group, ::Nothing)\n\nDrop the methods of group that Julia generated for positional default values, keeping the method that takes every argument of definition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.collapse_defaults"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.comparemethods-Tuple{Method, Method}","page":"Internals","text":"comparemethods(a, b)\n\nCompare methods a and b by file and line number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.comparemethods"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.constructor_methods-Tuple{UnionAll, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"constructor_methods(func, typesig)\n\nThe methods of Type{T}, for T any instance of the parametric type func, whose arguments match typesig. These are the constructors written as T{P}(...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.constructor_methods"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.constructor_parameters-Tuple{Method}","page":"Internals","text":"constructor_parameters(method)\n\nThe type parameters written in a constructor call, such as [T] for Point{T}(x) where {T}, or nothing when method is not a constructor written with parameters. Julia stores such a method under Type{Point{T}}, so methods(Point) leaves it out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.constructor_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.definition_arguments-Tuple{Expr, Bool}","page":"Internals","text":"definition_arguments(expr, show_defaults)\n\nFind the arguments of the method definition expr, with their default values when show_defaults is set. Returns nothing when expr does not define a method, or when the arguments would not change what a signature prints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.definition_arguments"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.documented_groups-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"documented_groups(groups, doc)\n\nThe group in groups that the docstring doc documents: the first defined at or after the docstring in its file. The docsystem adds a Tuple{T} to the type signature for each where T, which can match a method of another definition. All of groups when doc records no location or no group follows it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.documented_groups"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.expanded_vararg-Tuple{Method}","page":"Internals","text":"expanded_vararg(method)\n\nWhether method takes xs::Vararg{T,n}, which Julia writes into method.sig as n arguments of type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.expanded_vararg"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.find_tuples-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"find_tuples(typesig)\n\nConverts a method signature (or a union of several signatures) in a vector of (single) signatures.\n\nThis is used for decoding the method signature that a docstring is paired with. In the case when the docstring applies to multiple methods (e.g. when default positional argument values are used and define multiple methods at once), they are combined together as union of Tuple types.\n\njulia> DocStringExtensions.find_tuples(Tuple{String,Number,Int})\n1-element Vector{DataType}:\n Tuple{String, Number, Int64}\n\njulia> DocStringExtensions.find_tuples(Tuple{T} where T <: Integer)\n1-element Vector{UnionAll}:\n Tuple{T} where T<:Integer\n\njulia> s = Union{\n         Tuple{Int64},\n         Tuple{U},\n         Tuple{T},\n         Tuple{Int64,T},\n         Tuple{Int64,T,U}\n       } where U where T;\n\njulia> DocStringExtensions.find_tuples(s)\n5-element Vector{Type}:\n Tuple{Int64}\n Tuple{U} where U\n Tuple{T} where T\n Tuple{Int64, T} where T\n Tuple{Int64, T, U} where {T, U}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.find_tuples"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.format-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"format(abbr, buf, doc)\n\nExpand the Abbreviation abbr in the context of the DocStr doc and write the resulting markdown-formatted text to the IOBuffer buf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.format"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.getmethods!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"getmethods!(results, f, sig)\n\nA helper method for getmethods that collects methods in results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.getmethods!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.getmethods-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"getmethods(f, sig)\n\nCollect and return all methods of function f matching signature sig.\n\nThis is similar to methods(f, sig), but handles type signatures found in DocStr objects more consistently than methods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.getmethods"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.groupby!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"groupby!(f, groups, data)\n\nA helper method for groupby that uses a pre-allocated groups Dict.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.groupby!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.groupby-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"groupby(f, K, V, data)\n\nGroup data using function f where key type is specified by K and group type by V.\n\nThe function f takes a single argument, an element of data, and should return a 2-tuple of (computed_key, element). See the example below for details.\n\nExamples\n\ngroupby(Int, Vector{Int}, collect(1:10)) do num\n    mod(num, 3), num\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.groupby"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.interpolation-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"interpolation(object::T, captured::Expr) -> new_object\n\nInterface method for hooking into interpolation within docstrings to change the behaviour of the interpolation. object is the interpolated object within a docstring and captured is the raw expression that is documented by the docstring in which the interpolated object has been included.\n\nTo define custom behaviour for your own object types implement a method of interpolation(::T, captured) for type T and return a new_object to be interpolated into the final docstring. Note that you must own the definition of type T. new_object does not need to be of type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.interpolation"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.keywords-Tuple{Any, Method}","page":"Internals","text":"keywords(func, m)\n\nReturns the list of keywords for a particular method m of a function func.\n\nExamples\n\nf(x; a = 1, b...) = x\nkws = keywords(f, first(methods(f)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.keywords"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.matched_methods-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"matched_methods(tt)\n\nThe methods matching the full call signature tt, a Tuple type of the callable's type and the argument types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.matched_methods"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.matches_definition-Tuple{Method, Nothing}","page":"Internals","text":"matches_definition(::Method, ::Nothing)\n\nWhether method takes the positional arguments of definition, or a leading subset of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.matches_definition"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.method_length_over_limit-Tuple{Int64}","page":"Internals","text":"method_length_over_limit(len)\n\nDecides whether a length of method is too big to be visually appealing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.method_length_over_limit"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.method_return_type-Tuple{Any, Method, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"method_return_type(func, method, typesig)\n\nThe inferred return type of method for argument types typesig, as a vector holding one type, or an empty vector when method does not match typesig. Base.return_types infers every method that matches, so a more specific method whose signature overlaps method is skipped by its position in methods(func, typesig).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.method_return_type"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.methodgroups-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"methodgroups(func, typesig, modname; exact)\n\nGroup all methods of function func with type signatures typesig in module modname. Keyword argument exact = true matches signatures \"exactly\" with == rather than <:.\n\nExamples\n\ngroups = methodgroups(f, Union{Tuple{Any}, Tuple{Any, Integer}}, Main; exact = false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.methodgroups"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.parsedocs-Tuple{Module}","page":"Internals","text":"parsedocs(mod)\n\nParse all docstrings defined within a module mod.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.parsedocs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.printmethod-Tuple{IOBuffer, Base.Docs.Binding, Any, Method, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"printmethod(\n    buffer::IOBuffer,\n    binding::Base.Docs.Binding,\n    func,\n    method::Method,\n    typesig;\n    print_return_types,\n    definition\n) -> IOBuffer\n\nPrint a method signature to buffer with the argument types from typesig, followed by the inferred return type when print_return_types is set. Type parameters keep their names and are declared in a where clause after the arguments. Default values are left out unless definition gives them.\n\ndefinition is as for the untyped printmethod.\n\nExamples\n\nf(x::Int; a = 1, b...) = x\nsig = printmethod(Docs.Binding(Main, :f), f, first(methods(f)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.printmethod"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.printmethod-Tuple{IOBuffer, Base.Docs.Binding, Any, Method}","page":"Internals","text":"printmethod(buffer, binding, func, method; definition)\n\nPrint a simplified representation of a method signature to buffer. Some of these simplifications include:\n\nno TypeVars;\nno types, except ::T for an unnamed argument of type T;\nno default values, unless definition gives them;\n_ printed for untyped unnamed arguments, and for destructured arguments unless definition gives them.\n\ndefinition is the DefinitionArguments of the documented method definition, or nothing.\n\nExamples\n\nf(x; a = 1, b...) = x\nsig = printmethod(Docs.Binding(Main, :f), f, first(methods(f)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.printmethod"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.sourcepath-Tuple{AbstractString, Module}","page":"Internals","text":"sourcepath(file, mod)\n\nThe path of file relative to the root of the package that defines module mod, with / separators. Falls back to cleanpath when mod is not part of a package or file is outside it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.sourcepath"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.url-Tuple{Method}","page":"Internals","text":"url(m)\n\nGet the URL (file and line number) where a method m is defined.\n\nNote that this is based on the implementation of Base.url, but handles URLs correctly on TravisCI as well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.url"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.vararg_length-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"vararg_length(t)\n\nThe n of a Vararg{T,n} type, an Int or a method's type parameter, or nothing when the vararg has no count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.vararg_length"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.Abbreviation","page":"Internals","text":"Abbreviation objects are used to automatically generate context-dependent markdown content within documentation strings. Objects of this type interpolated into docstrings will be expanded automatically before parsing the text to markdown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.Abbreviation"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.DefinitionArguments","page":"Internals","text":"The positional and keyword arguments of a documented method definition, rendered as source text.\n\npositional has a (name, default) entry for each positional argument, in order. name is the argument's name, the destructuring of a destructured argument such as \"(a, b)\", or \"_\" for an unnamed argument. default is nothing when the argument has no default. keywords maps the name of each keyword argument with a default to that default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.DefinitionArguments"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.DocStringTemplate","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for DOCSTRING abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.DocStringTemplate"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.EnumInstances","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for INSTANCES abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.EnumInstances"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.FunctionName","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for FUNCTIONNAME abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.FunctionName"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.License","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for LICENSE abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.License"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.MethodList","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for METHODLIST abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.MethodList"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.MethodSignatures","page":"Internals","text":"The type for SIGNATURES abbreviations.\n\nshow_defaults\ndefinition\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.MethodSignatures"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.ModuleExports","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for EXPORTS abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.ModuleExports"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.ModuleImports","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for IMPORTS abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.ModuleImports"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.ModulePublic","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for PUBLIC abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.ModulePublic"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.Readme","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for README abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.Readme"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.Template","page":"Internals","text":"Internal abbreviation type used to wrap templated docstrings.\n\nLocation is a Symbol, either :before or :after. dict stores a reference to a module's templates. expr is the documented expression, which is passed to interpolation for each part of the template.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.Template"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.TypeDefinition","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for TYPEDEF abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.TypeDefinition"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.TypeFields","page":"Internals","text":"The type for FIELDS abbreviations.\n\ntypes\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.TypeFields"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.TypeSubtypes","page":"Internals","text":"The singleton type for SUBTYPES abbreviations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.TypeSubtypes"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.TypedMethodList","page":"Internals","text":"The type for TYPEDMETHODLIST abbreviations.\n\nreturn_types\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.TypedMethodList"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DocStringExtensions.TypedMethodSignatures","page":"Internals","text":"The type for TYPEDSIGNATURES abbreviations.\n\nreturn_types\nshow_defaults\ndefinition\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.TypedMethodSignatures"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/enums/#reference-enums","page":"Enums","text":"The examples show this abbreviation in a docstring, with the source it renders from.","title":"Enums"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/enums/#DocStringExtensions.INSTANCES","page":"Enums","text":"An Abbreviation that lists the instances of an enum with their values. It supports enums defined with @enum, and with @enumx from EnumX.jl, whose instances it qualifies with the enum's module name. It prints nothing for other objects.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       A fruit.\n       $(INSTANCES)\n       \"\"\"\n       @enum Fruit apple banana = 3 cherry\n\njulia> @doc Fruit\n  A fruit.\n\n    •  apple = 0\n    •  banana = 3\n    •  cherry = 4\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.INSTANCES"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/types/#examples-types","page":"Types","text":"The docstrings for these abbreviations are in the reference.\n\n\"\"\"\n[`TYPEDEF`](@ref) prints the type's header:\n\n$(TYPEDEF)\n\n---\n\n[`FIELDS`](@ref) lists the fields, with the docstring of each field that has one:\n\n$(FIELDS)\n\n---\n\n[`TYPEDFIELDS`](@ref) adds the field types:\n\n$(TYPEDFIELDS)\n\"\"\"\nstruct Foobar{T <: AbstractString}\n    \"Docstring for the `x` field.\"\n    x :: Nothing\n    y :: T # y has no docstring\n    \"Docstring for the `z` field.\"\n    z :: Vector{T}\nend\n\n\"\"\"\nAn interface for shapes. [`SUBTYPES`](@ref) lists the types that implement it:\n\n$(SUBTYPES)\n\"\"\"\nabstract type Shape end\n\n\"A circle.\"\nstruct Circle <: Shape end\n\n\"A square.\"\nstruct Square <: Shape end\n\n\n\n\nThe docstring renders as:\n\nFIELDS and TYPEDFIELDS work only on the fields of a type. For function arguments, see Function arguments.","title":"Types"},{"category":"type","location":"examples/types/#Examples.Foobar","page":"Types","text":"TYPEDEF prints the type's header:\n\nstruct Foobar{T<:AbstractString}\n\n\n\nFIELDS lists the fields, with the docstring of each field that has one:\n\nx: Docstring for the x field.\ny\nz: Docstring for the z field.\n\n\n\nTYPEDFIELDS adds the field types:\n\nx::Nothing: Docstring for the x field.\ny::AbstractString\nz::Vector{T} where T<:AbstractString: Docstring for the z field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.Foobar"},{"category":"type","location":"examples/types/#Examples.Shape","page":"Types","text":"An interface for shapes. SUBTYPES lists the types that implement it:\n\nCircle\nSquare\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.Shape"},{"category":"type","location":"examples/types/#Examples.Circle","page":"Types","text":"A circle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.Circle"},{"category":"type","location":"examples/types/#Examples.Square","page":"Types","text":"A square.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.Square"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/modules/#reference-modules","page":"Modules","text":"The examples show these abbreviations in a docstring, with the source it renders from.","title":"Modules"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/modules/#DocStringExtensions.EXPORTS","page":"Modules","text":"An Abbreviation that lists the exported names of a module as Documenter.jl @ref links.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       Shapes and their areas.\n       $(EXPORTS)\n       \"\"\"\n       module Shapes\n       using DocStringExtensions\n       export Circle, area\n       struct Circle end\n       area(::Circle) = π\n       end;\n\njulia> @doc Shapes\n  Shapes and their areas.\n\n    •  Circle\n    •  area\n\nnote: Note\nThe names are sorted alphabetically, ignoring a leading @, so macros sort in with the other names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.EXPORTS"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/modules/#DocStringExtensions.PUBLIC","page":"Modules","text":"An Abbreviation that lists the public names of a module, exported or declared public, as Documenter.jl @ref links. Before Julia 1.11, which added public, it lists the exported names.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       Shapes and their areas.\n       $(PUBLIC)\n       \"\"\"\n       module Shapes\n       using DocStringExtensions\n       export Circle, area\n       public perimeter\n       struct Circle end\n       area(::Circle) = π\n       perimeter(::Circle) = 2π\n       end;\n\njulia> @doc Shapes\n  Shapes and their areas.\n\n    •  Circle\n    •  area\n    •  perimeter\n\nnote: Note\nThe names are sorted alphabetically, ignoring a leading @, so macros sort in with the other names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.PUBLIC"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/modules/#DocStringExtensions.IMPORTS","page":"Modules","text":"An Abbreviation that lists, sorted by name, the modules a module loads with using. The list includes Base, which every module uses implicitly.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       Plotting helpers.\n       $(IMPORTS)\n       \"\"\"\n       module Plots\n       using DocStringExtensions, Markdown\n       end;\n\njulia> @doc Plots\n  Plotting helpers.\n\n    •  Base\n    •  DocStringExtensions\n    •  Markdown\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.IMPORTS"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/modules/#DocStringExtensions.README","page":"Modules","text":"An Abbreviation that includes the package README: the first file in the package root whose name contains readme, in any case. It prints nothing for a module that is not part of a package.\n\nnote: Note\nThe file is parsed as Julia Markdown, which differs from GitHub Markdown. For example, [text][] link shortcuts are not supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.README"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/modules/#DocStringExtensions.LICENSE","page":"Modules","text":"An Abbreviation that includes the package license: the first file in the package root whose name contains license, in any case. It prints nothing for a module that is not part of a package.\n\nnote: Note\nThe file is parsed as Julia Markdown, which differs from GitHub Markdown. For example, [text][] link shortcuts are not supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.LICENSE"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/templates/#reference-templates","page":"Templates","text":"The examples show templates applied to docstrings, with the source they render from.","title":"Templates"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/templates/#DocStringExtensions.@template","page":"Templates","text":"Defines a docstring template for a category, or a tuple of categories, of documented bindings in the current module.\n\nEach matching docstring that follows the @template is replaced by the template, with the original docstring placed where the template's DOCSTRING abbreviation is. Every template must contain DOCSTRING. Docstrings defined before the @template are left as they are, so define templates before the definitions they should apply to. A template applies only in the module that defines it. Submodules need their own, or a copy as shown below.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> module Geometry\n       using DocStringExtensions\n       @template (FUNCTIONS, METHODS) = \"\"\"\n           $(TYPEDSIGNATURES)\n           $(DOCSTRING)\n           \"\"\"\n       \"Compute the area of a square.\"\n       area(side::Int) = side^2\n       end;\n\njulia> @doc Geometry.area\n  area(side::Int64) -> Int64\n\n  Compute the area of a square.\n\nThe remaining examples show the other forms of @template.\n\n@template DEFAULT =\n    \"\"\"\n    $(SIGNATURES)\n    $(DOCSTRING)\n    \"\"\"\n\nDEFAULT applies to a docstring when no template for its own category exists.\n\n@template (FUNCTIONS, METHODS, MACROS) =\n    \"\"\"\n    $(SIGNATURES)\n    $(DOCSTRING)\n    $(METHODLIST)\n    \"\"\"\n\nA tuple of categories gives several categories the same template.\n\n@template MODULES = ModName\n\nA module name on the right copies that module's template for the same category. ModName must already define a MODULES template, so a module cannot copy a template from itself. To share templates across a package, define them in the parent module and copy them into each submodule:\n\nmodule Parent\nusing DocStringExtensions\n@template MODULES = \"\"\"\n    $(DOCSTRING)\n    $(EXPORTS)\n    \"\"\"\n\nmodule Child\nusing DocStringExtensions\n@template MODULES = Parent\nend\nend\n\nnote: Note\nSupported categories are DEFAULT, FUNCTIONS, METHODS, MACROS, TYPES, MODULES, and CONSTANTS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.@template"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/templates/#DocStringExtensions.DOCSTRING","page":"Templates","text":"An Abbreviation that marks where a @template places the original docstring. Every template must contain it.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> module Shapes\n       using DocStringExtensions\n       @template (FUNCTIONS, METHODS) = \"\"\"\n           $(DOCSTRING)\n           Part of the `Shapes` module.\n           \"\"\"\n       \"Compute the area of a square.\"\n       area(side) = side^2\n       end;\n\njulia> @doc Shapes.area\n  Compute the area of a square. Part of the Shapes module.\n\nwarning: Warning\nUse DOCSTRING only in templates. Showing an ordinary docstring that contains it throws an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.DOCSTRING"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/functions/#reference-functions","page":"Functions and methods","text":"The examples show these abbreviations in docstrings, with the source they render from.","title":"Functions and methods"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/functions/#DocStringExtensions.SIGNATURES","page":"Functions and methods","text":"An Abbreviation that prints the signatures of the documented method, with argument names and keywords but no types. See printmethod for the simplifications it applies.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       $(SIGNATURES)\n       Compute the area of a rectangle.\n       \"\"\"\n       area(width, height = width) = width * height;\n\njulia> @doc area\n  area(width)\n  area(width, height)\n\n  Compute the area of a rectangle.\n\ntip: Showing Default Values\nDefault values of positional and keyword arguments are omitted. To show them, use $(DocStringExtensions.MethodSignatures(defaults = true)). The methods that Julia generates for positional defaults are then printed as a single signature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.SIGNATURES"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/functions/#DocStringExtensions.TYPEDSIGNATURES","page":"Functions and methods","text":"An Abbreviation that prints the signatures of the documented method like SIGNATURES, with argument types and the inferred return type. See printmethod for the simplifications it applies.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       $(TYPEDSIGNATURES)\n       Compute the area of a rectangle.\n       \"\"\"\n       area(width::Int, height::Int) = width * height;\n\njulia> @doc area\n  area(width::Int64, height::Int64) -> Int64\n\n  Compute the area of a rectangle.\n\ntip: Disabling the Return Type\nThe return type is often inferred as Any. To leave it out, use $(DocStringExtensions.TypedMethodSignatures(false)).\n\ntip: Showing Default Values\nDefault values of positional and keyword arguments are omitted. To show them, pass defaults = true: $(DocStringExtensions.TypedMethodSignatures(true; defaults = true)). The methods that Julia generates for positional defaults are then printed as a single signature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.TYPEDSIGNATURES"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/functions/#DocStringExtensions.FUNCTIONNAME","page":"Functions and methods","text":"An Abbreviation that prints the name of the documented function. Use it in a signature written by hand, for example on a function with no methods yet.\n\nThe name is printed as plain text. Indent it or wrap it in backticks to format it as code.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       `$(FUNCTIONNAME)(d, θ)` calculates the log density of `d` at `θ`.\n       \"\"\"\n       function logdensity end;\n\njulia> @doc logdensity\n  logdensity(d, θ) calculates the log density of d at θ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.FUNCTIONNAME"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/functions/#DocStringExtensions.METHODLIST","page":"Functions and methods","text":"An Abbreviation that lists the methods of the documented function or type that are defined in the current module. Each method shows its signature and a link to the line that defines it.\n\nExamples\n\nMethods defined in the REPL have no file to link to, so the list below shows only their signatures.\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       Scale `x` by a factor.\n       $(METHODLIST)\n       \"\"\"\n       function scale end;\n\njulia> scale(x) = 2x;\n\njulia> scale(x, factor) = factor * x;\n\njulia> @doc scale\n  Scale x by a factor.\n\n  scale(x, factor)\n  scale(x)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.METHODLIST"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/functions/#DocStringExtensions.TYPEDMETHODLIST","page":"Functions and methods","text":"An Abbreviation that lists methods like METHODLIST, with argument types and the inferred return type in each signature.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       Scale `x` by a factor.\n       $(TYPEDMETHODLIST)\n       \"\"\"\n       function scale end;\n\njulia> scale(x::Int) = 2x;\n\njulia> scale(x::Float64, factor::Float64) = factor * x;\n\njulia> @doc scale\n  Scale x by a factor.\n\n  scale(x::Float64, factor::Float64) -> Float64\n  scale(x::Int64) -> Int64\n\ntip: Disabling the Return Type\nThe return type is often inferred as Any. To leave it out, use $(DocStringExtensions.TypedMethodList(false)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.TYPEDMETHODLIST"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/templates/#examples-templates","page":"Templates","text":"The docstrings for @template and DOCSTRING are in the reference.\n\n@template wraps every later docstring of a given kind in a module, so the abbreviations are written once. DOCSTRING marks where each original docstring goes.\n\nA template applies only to docstrings that come after it, so define templates before the definitions that use them.\nA template applies only in the module that defines it. A submodule needs its own, or a copy made with @template CATEGORY = ParentModule.\n\nmodule Geometry\nusing DocStringExtensions\n\n\"\"\"\nThis docstring comes before the `@template` definitions below, so it renders as written.\n\"\"\"\nunit_circle() = Circle(1.0)\n\n@template (FUNCTIONS, METHODS) = \"\"\"\n    $(TYPEDSIGNATURES)\n    $(DOCSTRING)\n    \"\"\"\n\n@template TYPES = \"\"\"\n    $(TYPEDEF)\n    $(DOCSTRING)\n    $(TYPEDFIELDS)\n    \"\"\"\n\n\"\"\"\nThis docstring holds only this sentence. The template adds the definition above it and the\nfields below it.\n\"\"\"\nstruct Circle\n    \"The distance from the centre to the edge.\"\n    radius::Float64\nend\n\n\"\"\"\nThis docstring holds only this sentence. The template adds the signature above it.\n\"\"\"\narea(c::Circle) = π * c.radius^2\n\nmodule Plain\nusing DocStringExtensions\nimport ..Geometry: Circle\n\n\"\"\"\n`Plain` is a submodule of `Geometry` but defines no template of its own, so this docstring\nrenders as written.\n\"\"\"\nperimeter(c::Circle) = 2π * c.radius\nend\n\nmodule Copied\nusing DocStringExtensions\nimport ..Geometry\nimport ..Geometry: Circle\n\n@template (FUNCTIONS, METHODS) = Geometry\n\n\"\"\"\n`Copied` copies the function template from `Geometry`, so the signature is added here too.\n\"\"\"\ndiameter(c::Circle) = 2 * c.radius\nend\n\nend\n\n\n\n\nThe docstrings render as:","title":"Templates"},{"category":"function","location":"examples/templates/#Examples.Geometry.unit_circle","page":"Templates","text":"This docstring comes before the @template definitions below, so it renders as written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.Geometry.unit_circle"},{"category":"type","location":"examples/templates/#Examples.Geometry.Circle","page":"Templates","text":"struct Circle\n\nThis docstring holds only this sentence. The template adds the definition above it and the fields below it.\n\nradius::Float64: The distance from the centre to the edge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.Geometry.Circle"},{"category":"function","location":"examples/templates/#Examples.Geometry.area","page":"Templates","text":"area(c::Examples.Geometry.Circle) -> Float64\n\nThis docstring holds only this sentence. The template adds the signature above it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.Geometry.area"},{"category":"function","location":"examples/templates/#Examples.Geometry.Plain.perimeter","page":"Templates","text":"Plain is a submodule of Geometry but defines no template of its own, so this docstring renders as written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.Geometry.Plain.perimeter"},{"category":"function","location":"examples/templates/#Examples.Geometry.Copied.diameter","page":"Templates","text":"diameter(c::Examples.Geometry.Circle) -> Float64\n\nCopied copies the function template from Geometry, so the signature is added here too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.Geometry.Copied.diameter"},{"category":"section","location":"setup/#setup","page":"Setup","text":"This guide adds DocStringExtensions to an existing package and covers the choices that depend on how the package is laid out.","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"setup/#Installation","page":"Setup","text":"Add DocStringExtensions as a dependency of the package itself. The package builds its docstrings when it loads, so the docs environment alone is not enough:\n\npkg> add DocStringExtensions","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"setup/#Abbreviations","page":"Setup","text":"Load the package in the module whose docstrings use it, before the first such docstring, then interpolate an abbreviation where its output should go:\n\nmodule MyPackage\n\nusing DocStringExtensions\n\n\"\"\"\n$(TYPEDSIGNATURES)\n\nCompute the area of a circle with radius `r`.\n\"\"\"\narea(r::Float64) = π * r^2\n\nend\n\nThe examples show what each abbreviation renders.","title":"Abbreviations"},{"category":"section","location":"setup/#Templates","page":"Setup","text":"To add the same abbreviations to every function or type docstring, define a @template. A template applies only to docstrings that come after it, so put it before the definitions it should document:\n\nmodule MyPackage\n\nusing DocStringExtensions\n\n@template (FUNCTIONS, METHODS, MACROS) = \"\"\"\n    $(TYPEDSIGNATURES)\n    $(DOCSTRING)\n    \"\"\"\n\n@template TYPES = \"\"\"\n    $(TYPEDEF)\n    $(DOCSTRING)\n    $(TYPEDFIELDS)\n    \"\"\"\n\ninclude(\"shapes.jl\")\ninclude(\"areas.jl\")\n\nend\n\nA template applies only in the module that defines it. If the package has submodules, copy the templates into each one:\n\nmodule Shapes\n\nusing DocStringExtensions\nimport ..MyPackage\n\n@template (FUNCTIONS, METHODS, MACROS) = MyPackage\n@template TYPES = MyPackage\n\nend\n\nThe template examples show the rendered result, including a submodule without a template.","title":"Templates"},{"category":"section","location":"setup/#Documenter","page":"Setup","text":"Abbreviations expand when Documenter renders a docstring, so a @docs or @autodocs block needs no extra setup.\n\nEXPORTS and PUBLIC write each name as a Documenter @ref link. Documenter fails the build when a link has no target, so every listed name needs its docstring included somewhere in the manual.","title":"Documenter"},{"category":"section","location":"setup/#Function-arguments","page":"Setup","text":"No abbreviation documents the arguments of a function. FIELDS and TYPEDFIELDS work only on the fields of a type. List arguments by hand under an # Arguments heading.","title":"Function arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"#DocStringExtensions","page":"Home","text":"To start using the package, see Setup.","title":"DocStringExtensions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Abbreviations","page":"Home","text":"Abbreviation Examples Reference\nSIGNATURES, TYPEDSIGNATURES, FUNCTIONNAME Functions and methods Functions and methods\nMETHODLIST, TYPEDMETHODLIST Functions and methods Functions and methods\nTYPEDEF, FIELDS, TYPEDFIELDS, SUBTYPES Types Types\nINSTANCES Enums Enums\nEXPORTS, PUBLIC, IMPORTS, README, LICENSE Modules Modules\n@template, DOCSTRING Templates Templates","title":"Abbreviations"},{"category":"module","location":"#DocStringExtensions","page":"Home","text":"Extensions for the Julia docsystem.\n\nDocStringExtensions generates parts of a docstring from the code it documents, so the docstring stays in step with the code. You interpolate an abbreviation into a docstring, and it expands to Markdown whenever the docstring is shown, in the REPL's ? mode or in Documenter.jl. Abbreviations cover method signatures and method lists, struct fields and subtypes, enum instances, and module exports. @template adds abbreviations to every docstring of a given kind in a module.\n\nExamples\n\nWhen the docstring is shown, $(SIGNATURES) is replaced by the signature:\n\njulia> using DocStringExtensions\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       A short summary of `func`.\n       $(SIGNATURES)\n       where `x` and `y` should both be positive.\n       \"\"\"\n       func(x, y) = x + y;\n\njulia> @doc func\n  A short summary of func.\n\n  func(x, y)\n\n  where x and y should both be positive.\n\nArgument names written into the text by hand, such as x and y above, are not updated.\n\nExports\n\nDOCSTRING\nEXPORTS\nFIELDS\nFUNCTIONNAME\nIMPORTS\nINSTANCES\nLICENSE\nMETHODLIST\nPUBLIC\nREADME\nSIGNATURES\nSUBTYPES\nTYPEDEF\nTYPEDFIELDS\nTYPEDMETHODLIST\nTYPEDSIGNATURES\n@template\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/modules/#examples-modules","page":"Modules","text":"The docstrings for these abbreviations are in the reference.\n\nThis is the Examples module that every example page draws on:\n\n\"\"\"\nThis docstring belongs to the `Examples` module.\n\n[`EXPORTS`](@ref) lists the exported names:\n\n$(EXPORTS)\n\n[`PUBLIC`](@ref) also lists the names declared `public`, here `bar` and `Fruit`:\n\n$(PUBLIC)\n\n[`IMPORTS`](@ref) lists the modules loaded with `using`:\n\n$(IMPORTS)\n\n[`README`](@ref) includes the package README, shown between the rules:\n\n---\n\n$(README)\n\n---\n\n[`LICENSE`](@ref) includes the package license the same way:\n\n---\n\n$(LICENSE)\n\n---\n\"\"\"\nmodule Examples\nusing DocStringExtensions\n\nexport foo, Foobar\npublic bar, Fruit\n\ninclude(\"functions.jl\")\ninclude(\"parameters.jl\")\ninclude(\"types.jl\")\ninclude(\"enums.jl\")\ninclude(\"templates.jl\")\n\nend\n\n\n\n\nThe docstring renders as:","title":"Modules"},{"category":"module","location":"examples/modules/#Examples","page":"Modules","text":"This docstring belongs to the Examples module.\n\nEXPORTS lists the exported names:\n\nFoobar\nfoo\n\nPUBLIC also lists the names declared public, here bar and Fruit:\n\nFoobar\nFruit\nbar\nfoo\n\nIMPORTS lists the modules loaded with using:\n\nBase\nDocStringExtensions\n\nREADME includes the package README, shown between the rules:\n\n\n\nExamples.jl\n\nThe demo library for DocStringExtensions.\n\n\n\nLICENSE includes the package license the same way:\n\n\n\nThe Examples package stands in for a real one. A real package would carry its license text in this file.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/types/#reference-types","page":"Types","text":"The examples show these abbreviations in docstrings, with the source they render from.","title":"Types"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/types/#DocStringExtensions.TYPEDEF","page":"Types","text":"An Abbreviation that prints the header of a type definition: struct, mutable struct, abstract type or primitive type, then the unqualified name, the type parameters, and the supertype unless it is Any. A primitive type also shows its size in bits.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       $(TYPEDEF)\n       A point in the plane.\n       \"\"\"\n       struct Point{T<:Real} <: Number\n           x::T\n           y::T\n       end\n\njulia> @doc Point\n  struct Point{T<:Real} <: Number\n\n  A point in the plane.\n\nnote: Note\nThe fields are not printed. Use FIELDS or TYPEDFIELDS for those.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.TYPEDEF"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/types/#DocStringExtensions.FIELDS","page":"Types","text":"An Abbreviation that lists the fields of a type, with the docstring of each field that has one.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       A point in the plane.\n       $(FIELDS)\n       \"\"\"\n       struct Point\n           \"Horizontal coordinate.\"\n           x::Float64\n           \"Vertical coordinate.\"\n           y::Float64\n           label::String\n       end\n\njulia> @doc Point\n  A point in the plane.\n\n    •  x: Horizontal coordinate.\n\n    •  y: Vertical coordinate.\n\n    •  label\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.FIELDS"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/types/#DocStringExtensions.TYPEDFIELDS","page":"Types","text":"An Abbreviation that lists the fields of a type like FIELDS, with the type of each field.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       A point in the plane.\n       $(TYPEDFIELDS)\n       \"\"\"\n       struct Point\n           \"Horizontal coordinate.\"\n           x::Float64\n           \"Vertical coordinate.\"\n           y::Float64\n           label::String\n       end\n\njulia> @doc Point\n  A point in the plane.\n\n    •  x::Float64: Horizontal coordinate.\n\n    •  y::Float64: Vertical coordinate.\n\n    •  label::String\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.TYPEDFIELDS"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/types/#DocStringExtensions.SUBTYPES","page":"Types","text":"An Abbreviation that lists the direct subtypes of a type as Documenter.jl @ref links. It prints nothing for other objects.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> \"\"\"\n       A shape.\n       $(SUBTYPES)\n       \"\"\"\n       abstract type Shape end\n\njulia> struct Circle <: Shape end\n\njulia> struct Square <: Shape end\n\njulia> @doc Shape\n  A shape.\n\n    •  Circle\n    •  Square\n\nnote: Note\nOnly subtypes defined in the type's own module, or in a module inside it, are listed, so a subtype another package defines is left out. A subtype in an inner module is qualified with that module's name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DocStringExtensions.SUBTYPES"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/enums/#examples-enums","page":"Enums","text":"The docstring for this abbreviation is in the reference.\n\n\"\"\"\n[`INSTANCES`](@ref) lists the instances of an enum with their values:\n\n$(INSTANCES)\n\"\"\"\n@enum Fruit apple banana = 3 cherry\n\n\n\n\nThe docstring renders as:","title":"Enums"},{"category":"type","location":"examples/enums/#Examples.Fruit","page":"Enums","text":"INSTANCES lists the instances of an enum with their values:\n\napple = 0\nbanana = 3\ncherry = 4\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Examples.Fruit"}]
}
